# Line endings.
#
# Every text file in this repository is stored with LF. `text=auto` makes that
# explicit and keeps it true regardless of a contributor's `core.autocrlf`; Git
# still decides the *working-tree* ending per platform, so this changes nothing
# for an existing checkout (verified: `git add --renormalize .` is a no-op here).
* text=auto

# `src/routeTree.gen.ts` is regenerated by @tanstack/router-plugin on every
# `vite dev` / `vite build`. The generator reads the file, compares it byte for
# byte with the text it produced, and writes with node's `fs.writeFile` — LF
# only, on every OS (router-generator 1.167.21, `generator.js`:
# `if (this.routeTreeFileCache.fileContent === routeTreeContent) {} else safeFileWrite(...)`).
#
# On a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true` the working copy is CRLF, so
# it never matches: the dev server rewrites the file to LF, Git then reports it
# as modified *with an empty diff* (Git expected CRLF, the clean filter makes
# the content identical), and every `git pull` that touches the file aborts with
#
#   error: Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge:
#           src/routeTree.gen.ts
#
# `eol=lf` makes the checkout match what the generator writes, so the file stops
# going dirty. This is safe precisely because the file is generated: discarding a
# local copy loses nothing — `git checkout -- src/routeTree.gen.ts`, or any
# dev/build run, reproduces the committed bytes exactly.
src/routeTree.gen.ts text eol=lf

# Binary — never convert. Git auto-detects both of these already; stated so the
# intent is explicit and a future `.zip`/icon cannot be normalised by accident.
*.zip -text
*.ico -text
